# FreeLinX patches for NetBSD networking tools (net/)

This directory holds FreeLinX-specific patches applied on top of the pristine
NetBSD 10.1 source. It contains ONLY small, isolated, documented changes — the
goal is to compile and link these NetBSD tools against the FreeLinX musl
toolchain and the Linux kernel. See also shells/netbsd-sh/patches/README for
the shared musl-compat strategy (base/compat shims for cdefs/getprogname/
strlcpy/err etc.) that every NetBSD-derived port reuses.

## Per-port status and required work

### net/netbsd-ping (sbin/ping) — scaffolded, needs header shims
NetBSD's ping is mostly portable (raw ICMP sockets exist on Linux), but it
includes NetBSD-kernel headers musl/Linux do not ship:
  - <netinet/in_systm.h>        (n_time/n_short types)   -> map to musl <netinet/in.h>
  - <netinet/ip_var.h>          (NetBSD-only)            -> provide minimal stub or drop
  - <netipsec/ipsec.h>          (IPsec, NetBSD-only)     -> disable (CONFIG/IPsec absent)
  - BSD <sys/cdefs.h>, err(3)   -> base/compat
Patches needed: replace those includes, samin from netbsd-sh.

### net/netbsd-ifconfig (sbin/ifconfig) — blocked on kernel API
This is not a header-only port. NetBSD's ifconfig drives interfaces and
addresses through **routing sockets** (AF_ROUTE / struct rt_msghdr /
RTM_* messages) and NetBSD-only ioctls/MIBs (SIOCGIFMEDIA, sysctl NET_RT_*).
The Linux kernel has no AF_ROUTE; it uses AF_NETLINK (RTM_NEWLINK /
RTM_NEWADDR). Porting requires rewriting ifconfig_hostops to talk netlink +
Linux ioctls — a Linux hostops layer, not a cosmetic patch. Until that is
written (in patches/), this port cannot link.

### net/netbsd-route (sbin/route) — blocked on kernel API
NetBSD's route is fundamentally a routing-socket client (RTM_ADD / RTM_DELETE /
RTM_CHANGE). Linux substitutes AF_NETLINK RTM_NEWROUTE/RTM_DELROUTE with a
different wire format. Requires rewriting route_hostops onto netlink. Same
caveat as ifconfig.

## Deciding principle (from the ports README)
For every missing libc API: is it POSIX? -> does musl provide it? -> does the
program actually need it? Only then patch. For missing *kernel* APIs (AF_ROUTE,
NetBSD MIBs), the answer is a Linux hostops port — that is the substantive work
these two scaffolds exist to host.
